From 7ce4a8fa7a8dc218479879a4e12310f984ec6815 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Noel Blatter <noel.blatter@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 09:42:59 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Start-up: the C library's heap grows in large steps and keeps
 its blocks

glibc's malloc asks the kernel for every block above 128 kB separately and hands it
back on free, and it grows its heap 128 kB at a time. A benchmark run allocates and
frees arrays of a few hundred kB to a few MB all the time, each of them new memory the
kernel has to clear and map page by page: about 20 000 page faults in one run. With
HEAP_IN_LARGE_STEPS blocks up to 32 MB come from the heap, where a freed block is
reused, the heap grows by 256 MB of address space at a time and is not trimmed. With
HEAP_HUGE_PAGES the kernel is told that the heap may use 2 MB pages (MADV_HUGEPAGE),
which is what counts where transparent huge pages are set to madvise. glibc only; on
any other C library the measure reports that it is off. Both can be switched per
process with DIFFEROMETOR_STARTUP (heap, hugepages).

Measured on max/next 67f0055, arm64 Linux in Docker with one CPU, twelve fresh runs in
turn on a machine in use, minimum (median): grid first run 166.3 (174.9) -> 153.4
(164.7) ms, forward 30.7 -> 25.2 ms; with transparent huge pages switched off for the
process 155.4 (161.7) ms. aLIGO unchanged (127.7 -> 129.3 ms). A second block of 8 MB
after a freed one: 514 page faults -> 0. Not measured on the Xeon: the gain depends on
its huge page setting and on what a page fault costs there.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../__pycache__/_startup.cpython-312.pyc      | Bin 63725 -> 66462 bytes
 differometor/_startup.py                      |  71 +++++++++++++++++-
 2 files changed, 70 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

